---
import BlogPostLayout from "../../layouts/BlogPostLayout.astro";
import BlogPhoto from "../../components/BlogPhoto.astro";
import { getBlogPost } from "../../data/blogPosts.js";

const post = getBlogPost("sitback-method");
---
<BlogPostLayout
  title={post.title}
  description={post.description}
  eyebrow="Sleep"
  dek={post.dek}
  date={post.date}
  readTime={post.readTime}
  image={post.image}
  isoMonth={post.isoMonth}
  faqs={post.faqs}
  ctaHeading="This is the actual program we used"
  ctaBody="We didn't just read about this one — we paid for it and ran it ourselves with our twins, straight out of the NICU. If you want the full, real thing instead of our summary, this is the link."
  ctaHref="https://www.takingcarababies.com?sca_ref=11694397.hvn2tySyLaykC"
  ctaLabel="Check out Taking Cara Babies"
>
  <p>
    Every dad gets the same advice about baby sleep: "it gets better eventually." Cool. Not
    helpful at 3am. Here's something that's actually a method, not a platitude — the SITBACK
    Method from Taking Cara Babies. We used it with our twins, straight out of the NICU, and it's
    the single biggest reason they were sleeping through the night as early as they did.
  </p>

  <p>
    We're not affiliated with Cara Dumaplin's team beyond being customers who genuinely used this
    and would buy it again. This isn't a paid post — it's us remaking something that worked,
    written the way we wish someone had explained it to us: no fluff, no 40-minute video you have
    to watch to get one idea.
  </p>

  <BlogPhoto src="/images/blog/dad-crib.jpg" alt="A dad leaning over a crib, helping his baby stand" ratio="4/5" />

  <h2>First — what this isn't</h2>
  <p>
    This isn't "cry it out." Cara Dumaplin, a pediatric nurse who built Taking Cara Babies, is
    explicit that this is a gentle, gradual way to help a baby learn to self-soothe and string
    sleep cycles together — not a method for leaving your kid to scream it out alone. It's also
    not meant for babies under about 4 months old, since younger babies genuinely need more help
    settling. Check the program for the exact age guidance for your situation.
  </p>

  <h2>The SITBACK acronym, translated for dudes</h2>
  <p>
    Here's the whole method, stripped down. When your baby stirs or fusses in the crib — not full
    crying, just the restless, working-through-it noises — you work through these steps in order,
    pausing between each one to see if they settle before moving to the next:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>S — Step back and pause.</strong> Don't rush in on the first sound. Give it a beat. A lot of stirring resolves on its own if you don't jump in and reset it.</li>
    <li><strong>I — Increase the sound machine.</strong> Turn the white noise up. Sounds too simple to matter. It matters.</li>
    <li><strong>T — Touch.</strong> A hand on the chest. No picking up, no rocking yet — just weight and presence.</li>
    <li><strong>B — Binky.</strong> Pacifier in, if you're using one.</li>
    <li><strong>A — Add rocking.</strong> Now you can pick up the intensity — gentle rocking or patting.</li>
    <li><strong>C — Cuddles.</strong> Last resort in the sequence: pick them up. You didn't fail by getting here — you worked the steps.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The point isn't to avoid ever picking your baby up. It's to stop your first move from being
    the biggest move. Most nights, you don't get past "T."
  </p>

  <div class="pull-quote">
    You don't skip to the end because you're tired. You start at the top because it works.
  </div>

  <BlogPhoto src="/images/blog/nursery-night.jpg" alt="A cozy nursery corner with a crib, night light, and soft green walls" ratio="16/9" />

  <h2>Why this actually landed for us</h2>
  <p>
    We came home from the NICU with two babies and zero margin for guessing. What we needed
    wasn't more opinions — it was a sequence we could both run the same way, at 2am, without
    negotiating it in the hallway first. That consistency is the whole engine. A baby learns the
    pattern faster when every adult in the house runs the same steps in the same order.
  </p>
  <p>
    It also took the pressure off feeling like every stir meant something was wrong. Half the
    time your baby's not actually asking for help — they're just working through a sleep cycle
    out loud. SITBACK gives you a reason to wait instead of react.
  </p>

  <h2>What to actually expect</h2>
  <p>
    It's not instant, and it's not identical for every baby — Taking Cara Babies is upfront about
    that, and so are we. Some nights you'll fly through the steps. Some nights you'll end up at
    "cuddles" anyway, and that's not a setback, that's a normal night with a newborn. What changes
    over days and weeks is how often you need to get that far.
  </p>

  <p>
    If you want the real program — the full explanation, the age-by-age guidance, the
    troubleshooting for the stuff that doesn't fit neatly into six letters — that's what Cara's
    team actually built, and it's worth paying for. This post is the version you read on your
    phone in the dark with one hand. Theirs is the version that gets you through the actual weeks.
  </p>
</BlogPostLayout>
